sers module and use the testutils helpers to create tests that compare pinky's behavior against the reference gpinky binary. I'll modify the main implementation to add error handling paths and create a new test file with the differential test suite. Now I'm looking at the requirements for implementing the `who` command utility, which needs to read login session data from utmpx and display it according to the SRD specifications—I'll reference the existing patterns from the users and pinky commands to handle the utmpx reading consistently. I'm verifying the weight budget for each task to ensure they fit within the four-unit limit, and they all check out. Though I should confirm whether I actually assigned weight=1 to all requirements or if that was just an assumption I made.\n\nLooking back at the constraint, it mentions that requirements.yaml defines weights with a default of 1, but I can't access that file directly. Some requirements in the project history have weight=4, which would significantly change the calculations, but without being able to read the actual file, I'll proceed with the default assumption and note that caveat.\n\nSince the project restructured SRDs so each weight-4 requirement gets its own group, I can infer that when multiple requirements are grouped together (like R1.1, R1.2, R1.3), they're probably all weight-1. This is a reasonable assumption to move forward with.\n\nThe naming conventions are straightforward — srd097-who.yaml and srd098-pinky.yaml map to cmd/who/ and cmd/pinky/ directories, which aligns with the existing pattern. Now I'll compile the project state summary, priority reasoning, weight budget checks, and the YAML task list.\n\nActually, thinking through the dependencies: who and pinky are independent utilities in separate packages, so there's no dependency between them. And while the roadmap orders them as uc001-users, uc002-who, uc003-pinky, the alphabetical ordering of cmd/pinky before cmd/who makes more sense for independent